00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.440
Let's talk a little
about a BigQuery.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:27.785
So why centralized data?

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:32.930
If you have all the
data in one place,

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:33.971
you can connect the dots.

00:02:33.971 --> 00:02:36.860
So if you have
datasets of newsletters

00:02:36.860 --> 00:02:40.040
and Google Analytics and
your content database,

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.910
you can easily make connections.

00:02:41.910 --> 00:02:46.070
Versus if they were sitting
in different databases,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:49.070
it becomes difficult. And
it's efficient for users

00:02:49.070 --> 00:02:52.010
to go into one place
to get that data.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:53.510
There's no duplication.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:02:55.400
You're not getting
reports from one place

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:58.280
and then two different
places and those numbers

00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.580
don't match up.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:01.400
And it's efficient
in data warehousing.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.380
You use the same
project management

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:09.390
product, engineering, and QA
and consolidate your efforts.

00:03:09.390 --> 00:03:11.560
And most importantly
relevant to this

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.760
talk is it's the
foundation for ML.

00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:20.330
And why did we use BigQuery?

00:03:20.330 --> 00:03:24.260
Before this, people used
to either pull reports

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:26.900
from different systems,
email them out.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:31.020
There were different databases,
different data warehouses.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.800
So a couple of years
ago, we thought

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:37.700
that we need to consolidate
everything into one database,

00:03:37.700 --> 00:03:38.970
and we chose BigQuery.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:41.290
And these are some of the
reasons why BigQuery was

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:41.790
[INAUDIBLE].

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.360
It's based on Dremel technology.

00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:46.550
And this stuff is
on their website--

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.980
terabytes in seconds
and petabytes in minutes

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:50.930
to be able to query that data.

00:03:50.930 --> 00:03:52.810
It's fully managed.

00:03:52.810 --> 00:03:56.870
SQL-- lots of people
know SQL, so easy

00:03:56.870 --> 00:03:58.940
to get people onto BigQuery.

00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:03.020
And now with yesterday's
release of BigQuery ML,

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.110
you can even do machine
learning on top of a BigQuery.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:14.200
So this is the technology
stack for our BI platform.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:19.329
We're using ETL, using Airflow,
BigQuery as our data warehouse,

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:20.560
and Looker.

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:22.660
And I think there have
been other talks talking

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.290
about how people have used
Looker with BigQuery to build

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:29.020
user journeys and stuff.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:30.430
So we're also doing that.

00:04:30.430 --> 00:04:32.680
That could be a separate
talk, but I'm just mentioning

00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.610
that we are also doing this.

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:39.520
And these are some of the data
sources we have in BigQuery--

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:44.950
Google Analytics, DoubleClick,
subscriptions, newsletters.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.120
And this is continuously
growing, the number of datasets

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:50.522
in our data warehouse.

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:55.070
So getting two machine learning.

00:04:58.600 --> 00:05:02.160
So I see machine learning in
two big buckets, one machine

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.520
learning that's prepackaged
that Google has done your data

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:08.400
science work for
you, and they've

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.000
exposed APIs to be able to do.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.720
And these are natural
language processing;

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:22.670
AutoML, which was also released
in this conference; speech;

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.290
video intelligence; and vision.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.300
And the other half is
where you're data science,

00:05:27.300 --> 00:05:30.180
and you're doing machine
learning in house

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:35.340
using TensorFlow/CloudML,
BigQuery ML,

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.300
and using DataProc and Spark.

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:45.490
So there was a case
study published back

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:47.620
in November of how
Hearst Newspapers is

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:49.960
using natural
language processing

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.780
and the different use cases.

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:56.020
So the two pieces that we've
used from natural language

00:05:56.020 --> 00:06:02.440
processing is to be able to
extract entities and categories

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:04.690
from our text.

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.810
There's a part of speech
APO that we haven't used,

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:13.360
but this alone, we have
about six use cases

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:14.770
using these features.

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.840
So I took an article from
"San Francisco Chronicle"--

00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:24.110
it's about this movie,
"Black Panther"--

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:27.635
and ran it through the
web interface from Google.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:32.720
And you can see that
it has correctly

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:37.130
detected "Black Panther"
as a work of art.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:39.140
It has a Wikipedia link.

00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:45.230
It also has the knowledge ID
and also the saliency score.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:48.020
And naturally, the
entities are broken down

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:50.030
into different categories.

00:06:50.030 --> 00:06:55.370
There is work of art, places,
organization, locations.

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:57.740
And then, that is
further broken down

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:00.350
into proper and improper nouns.

00:07:02.950 --> 00:07:07.000
And then, it also correctly
detected the right category

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:11.290
for it and also given
a confidence score.

00:07:15.570 --> 00:07:18.220
This is all good, but
how do we use this thing?

00:07:18.220 --> 00:07:21.200
It looks very simple.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.210
So I'm going to go through
some of these use cases

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:28.470
across as how to use Google NLP
from a simple use of displaying

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:32.640
these tags on our CMS system
to building a recommendation

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:37.650
system, and also matching
ads to the right content.

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:42.030
So I'll go one at a time.

00:07:42.030 --> 00:07:44.390
And this is sort of like
the high-level architecture

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:46.730
of how everything connects.

00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:52.340
So when content is ingested
into our CMS system,

00:07:52.340 --> 00:07:56.390
we make these NLP calls, get
the metadata back, and store it

00:07:56.390 --> 00:07:59.250
into our database.

00:07:59.250 --> 00:08:01.610
And then when the
article is rendered,

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:06.500
these tags are part of
the metatag of the HTML.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:10.100
And from that, we have a
third-party customer data

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:16.070
platform, which is extracting
these NLP tags into its system.

00:08:16.070 --> 00:08:19.400
And at the same time, there's
a JavaScript call that--

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:22.580
for-- to DoubleClick
for Publishers

00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:25.340
to be able to render ads.

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:27.700
And also, this data is
pushed into BigQuery.

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:31.490
Our CMS content, along
with the NLP tags,

00:08:31.490 --> 00:08:35.419
are also pushed into
BigQuery, upon which

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:39.340
we can do BI reporting
and then also build

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:41.169
a recommendation system on it.

00:08:45.410 --> 00:08:48.560
So first use case--
segmentation.

00:08:48.560 --> 00:08:52.730
Segmentation is that be able
to identify a group of users,

00:08:52.730 --> 00:08:56.660
say which group of
users are sports

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:01.250
readers versus like
food and wine readers.

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:04.220
Our CDB platform, it
has a built in mechanism

00:09:04.220 --> 00:09:08.720
to be able to use these
tags to create segments.

00:09:08.720 --> 00:09:11.660
And how the segments
are used is that you

00:09:11.660 --> 00:09:14.135
can push marketing messaging.

00:09:14.135 --> 00:09:18.410
Like, let's say if you know
there is a segment about people

00:09:18.410 --> 00:09:20.060
who like to read
tennis news, you

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:23.720
can push a newsletter
using this tool.

00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:30.620
The other use cases--

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:32.280
DFP ad targeting.

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:34.480
So I had mentioned
that we are making--

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.570
in our key value
pairs, we can pass

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:41.450
in a key of the NLP category
and the actual category that

00:09:41.450 --> 00:09:43.260
was for that page.

00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:46.790
So what this does is
DFP, over a month,

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:49.310
it can collect all this data.

00:09:49.310 --> 00:09:53.330
And then one can run
reporting on it telling how

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:57.470
users and how ads were
displayed across our content.

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.060
And then if another
customer says,

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:05.570
hey, I want to have a campaign
to target Olympics web pages;

00:10:05.570 --> 00:10:08.750
put my ad on Olympic
web pages, they

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:12.650
can create a campaign
with a certain criteria.

00:10:12.650 --> 00:10:16.760
And now this vendor's
ads or the partner's ads

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:20.600
can be displayed on
Olympics content.

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:26.635
So this is just a screenshot of
how the rules are set in DFP.

00:10:31.460 --> 00:10:38.780
Since this is a BI report,
it uses Google Analytics WCM

00:10:38.780 --> 00:10:45.300
content and NLP data to come
up with some useful numbers.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:49.470
Basically in this report, it's
showing for this category,

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.010
these are the number
of users who visited,

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:55.250
and these are the number of
articles in that category.

00:10:55.250 --> 00:10:57.890
And a simple ratio
could show that--

00:10:57.890 --> 00:11:00.770
a higher ratio
would mean that this

00:11:00.770 --> 00:11:03.940
is something editorial should
focus on this category,

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:07.250
write more content about it.

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:08.960
And there's numerous
different things

00:11:08.960 --> 00:11:11.630
you can do if you have
Google Analytics, NLP,

00:11:11.630 --> 00:11:14.760
and content data.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:16.640
For example, you can
create trend graphs

00:11:16.640 --> 00:11:22.310
about a certain topic or certain
personality, how their fame--

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:25.490
are they getting more famous
or less famous over the years,

00:11:25.490 --> 00:11:26.480
like content-wise?

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:33.060
And this is another
analysis of--

00:11:33.060 --> 00:11:36.450
so we get content
from third parties.

00:11:36.450 --> 00:11:39.840
Which third parties give
what type of content?

00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:41.520
Does this source
give us more sports

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:43.530
content versus the other one?

00:11:43.530 --> 00:11:45.840
And again, you can
build different kind

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:49.230
of reports using this.

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:55.970
So I'll get into
recommendation systems.

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.790
So why a recommendation
system important?

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.030
You might have read
Netflix values its--

00:12:05.030 --> 00:12:08.400
their recommendation
at $1 billion.

00:12:08.400 --> 00:12:11.880
So in our context, if we could
reuse older content which

00:12:11.880 --> 00:12:13.560
is just sitting
still and nobody's

00:12:13.560 --> 00:12:18.990
using it or looking at it,
this could increase engagement.

00:12:18.990 --> 00:12:21.970
People would stay on
the website longer,

00:12:21.970 --> 00:12:23.580
which means more ad revenue.

00:12:23.580 --> 00:12:28.050
And eventually, people
might even subscribe.

00:12:28.050 --> 00:12:30.570
Right now, people tend
to go to the home page

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:33.180
and whatever links
over there are--

00:12:33.180 --> 00:12:34.630
that's the content they can see.

00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:36.480
But if we can use our
recommendation system

00:12:36.480 --> 00:12:39.240
to explore older content,
that would be very useful.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:43.530
So these are three
different types

00:12:43.530 --> 00:12:47.221
of recommendation systems that
are supported by the Google

00:12:47.221 --> 00:12:47.970
Cloud [INAUDIBLE].

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:51.600
So we did a Content to
Content recommendation system,

00:12:51.600 --> 00:12:56.040
a personalized recommendation
system, and a Video

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:58.015
to Content
recommendation system.

00:13:03.630 --> 00:13:07.140
So since we had the NLP data
with the content data sitting

00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:10.560
in BigQuery, it was a
very low-hanging fruit

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.330
to build a
recommendation system.

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:16.290
And a core concept of
this is any two articles

00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:19.950
which have high
overlapping NLP entities

00:13:19.950 --> 00:13:21.990
are related to each other.

00:13:21.990 --> 00:13:26.280
And this is
essentially a big SQL

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:29.940
with certain rules and
conditions that recreate it.

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:34.020
And it runs on a periodic
basis, like every 20 minutes.

00:13:34.020 --> 00:13:38.130
As new content comes
in, we run this SQL.

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:41.820
Let me show the
diagram for this.

00:13:41.820 --> 00:13:46.470
We run that SQL, and BigQuery
stores that in Cloud SQL.

00:13:46.470 --> 00:13:50.850
And it's fronted by a
Kubernetes web service layer

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:52.800
to serve these recommendations.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:13:56.970
And from the front end,
there's a JavaScript call

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:59.560
to render these recommendations.

00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:02.720
And that API call, they
pass on the content ID.

00:14:02.720 --> 00:14:04.770
You find all the
related content that's

00:14:04.770 --> 00:14:07.650
already pre-computed in
Cloud SQL, a Postgres

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:10.910
database, and render down.

00:14:15.260 --> 00:14:20.440
So continuing on this
concept-- so this

00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:22.600
was actually a Hack Day idea.

00:14:22.600 --> 00:14:25.810
We had a Hack Day, and
I did this prototype

00:14:25.810 --> 00:14:29.590
to see if we could extract
anything useful from video.

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:34.330
So we took our videos,
convert, extracted the sound

00:14:34.330 --> 00:14:39.130
using a FFmpeg tool
that's open source,

00:14:39.130 --> 00:14:41.560
and used that sound
to make a call

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:43.780
to the Speech API,
which gives you

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:46.330
back the text for that video.

00:14:46.330 --> 00:14:49.420
And then again, you can
run NLP on that test,

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:53.710
as well as combine that with
the metadata for the video,

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:55.750
and you'll get NLP tags back.

00:14:58.510 --> 00:15:01.390
Again, just like
our text content,

00:15:01.390 --> 00:15:03.400
when we ingest
the video content,

00:15:03.400 --> 00:15:04.910
we're storing the meta--

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:08.860
the transcript as well as the
metadata tags along with it.

00:15:14.570 --> 00:15:15.710
Let me back up.

00:15:15.710 --> 00:15:17.420
And what we did was that--

00:15:17.420 --> 00:15:19.250
since we have these
NLP tags, this

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.290
powers another
recommendation system

00:15:21.290 --> 00:15:24.320
to recommend videos to text.

00:15:24.320 --> 00:15:26.690
Since our text
already has NLP tags,

00:15:26.690 --> 00:15:29.870
and we've extracted these
NLP tags from video,

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:33.320
now we can build an in-house
recommendation system.

00:15:33.320 --> 00:15:36.110
So this saved our
company some money.

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:38.630
Instead of buying a
video-to-text recommender,

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:43.190
we just build using NLP
and search technology

00:15:43.190 --> 00:15:45.450
to build our own
recommendation system.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:51.930
So this project we
worked in collaboration

00:15:51.930 --> 00:15:53.890
with our TV department.

00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:56.120
This is using TensorFlow.

00:15:58.740 --> 00:16:00.870
Talk a little bit about
what TensorFlow is.

00:16:00.870 --> 00:16:02.850
You might have already heard it.

00:16:02.850 --> 00:16:04.710
Neural networks are back.

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:07.080
And the current deep
learning revolution

00:16:07.080 --> 00:16:09.330
is because of deep
neural networks.

00:16:09.330 --> 00:16:11.190
They're bigger and hierarchical.

00:16:11.190 --> 00:16:14.430
And many of the Google
products, especially in the AI,

00:16:14.430 --> 00:16:16.440
are based on TensorFlow.

00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:21.059
And Cloud ML is a managed
version of TensorFlow.

00:16:27.550 --> 00:16:31.090
So we built a in-house
personalized recommendation

00:16:31.090 --> 00:16:32.230
system.

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:35.440
That could be like one whole
talk, talking about how

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:38.770
the algorithm and how it works
and the full architecture

00:16:38.770 --> 00:16:39.680
of it.

00:16:39.680 --> 00:16:42.530
But essentially in summary,
it's a using scalar-vector

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:46.660
decomposition, basically
a collaborative filtering

00:16:46.660 --> 00:16:47.710
algorithm.

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:49.450
And that algorithm
is something that you

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:53.890
can fit into TensorFlow,
because you can solve it

00:16:53.890 --> 00:16:56.920
through using gradient descent.

00:16:56.920 --> 00:17:00.970
And TensorFlow library
helps you solve

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:06.970
algorithms that can be put into
a gradient descent problem.

00:17:06.970 --> 00:17:11.920
And it's basically looking
at a user's history and also

00:17:11.920 --> 00:17:17.640
the history of people who
have similar taste to it.

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.650
And there's an open source
implementation Google

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:27.700
released using Google
Analytics, CMS content to build

00:17:27.700 --> 00:17:28.470
recommendations.

00:17:28.470 --> 00:17:30.625
And I'll encourage
you to take a look,

00:17:30.625 --> 00:17:32.470
and it's a bit similar
to what we did.

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:38.440
And the high-level
architecture-- as we

00:17:38.440 --> 00:17:43.220
were reading our content from
content and Google Analytics

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:45.530
data from BigQuery--
and you see there's

00:17:45.530 --> 00:17:49.370
another advantage that all the
data's sitting in BigQuery.

00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:56.160
Do some preprocessing, and
then run this TensorFlow model.

00:17:56.160 --> 00:18:00.040
And then this TensorFlow
model gets stored--

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:02.420
that's the output
of the TensorFlow,

00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:05.170
and it's fronted by
TensorFlow Serving.

00:18:05.170 --> 00:18:08.510
TensorFlow Serving
is a RPC layer

00:18:08.510 --> 00:18:14.990
that helps you deliver the
recommendations on this model.

00:18:14.990 --> 00:18:18.640
And then, it's fronted by a
RESTful web service layer.

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:27.770
Some of the other use
cases for a TensorFlow

00:18:27.770 --> 00:18:33.270
is to do propensity
modeling, forecasting,

00:18:33.270 --> 00:18:38.150
content virality prediction,
build customer content,

00:18:38.150 --> 00:18:39.850
classification of content.

00:18:39.850 --> 00:18:42.350
So I actually also
had churn modeling.

00:18:42.350 --> 00:18:45.190
And that's a use case that
I built in BigQuery ML,

00:18:45.190 --> 00:18:46.720
and I'll be talking about it.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:50.440
So now, Google
offers you a variety

00:18:50.440 --> 00:18:53.210
of ways of doing
machine learning.

00:18:53.210 --> 00:18:55.270
So it's a question
of, what's the most--

00:18:55.270 --> 00:18:57.640
what makes sense is--
you have to figure out

00:18:57.640 --> 00:18:59.320
what makes sense
to use TensorFlow

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:07.620
versus BigQuery ML versus
AutoML and all these other API.

00:19:07.620 --> 00:19:12.690
So I gave a talk about
BigQuery ML yesterday.

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:17.715
So why BigQuery ML
for Hearst Newspapers?

00:19:17.715 --> 00:19:20.310
As I already told
you that all our data

00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:21.660
is already sitting in BigQuery.

00:19:21.660 --> 00:19:23.640
So this was-- made
a lot of sense

00:19:23.640 --> 00:19:28.836
to just do this using BQML.

00:19:28.836 --> 00:19:33.660
It enables anyone familiar
with SQL to get on board

00:19:33.660 --> 00:19:36.720
and start doing
machine learning.

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:40.730
And the alternate would be
to first learn R or Python,

00:19:40.730 --> 00:19:45.710
learn a framework like
SciKit Learn or TensorFlow.

00:19:45.710 --> 00:19:47.460
But over here using
BQML, you don't

00:19:47.460 --> 00:19:50.820
need to learn any of those.

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:54.930
You don't need to ETL data out,
do a machine learning outside,

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:56.790
and then ETL stuff back in.

00:19:56.790 --> 00:19:59.190
Everything gets done in place.

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:06.150
And goodies, like normalization
and one-hot encoding,

00:20:06.150 --> 00:20:08.110
it just does it for you.

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:09.780
And then you have
other SQL syntax

00:20:09.780 --> 00:20:12.480
to get evaluation
of your machine

00:20:12.480 --> 00:20:14.990
learning model
right in BigQuery.

00:20:18.540 --> 00:20:20.610
So it's a relevant
churn prediction.

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:24.460
It's a relevant
use case for media.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:26.520
Because you might have
heard people have choices,

00:20:26.520 --> 00:20:32.010
and it's hard to keep
them-- keep the subscribers.

00:20:32.010 --> 00:20:37.350
So if we could figure out
a way or have some insight

00:20:37.350 --> 00:20:40.290
into the future of which
subscribers are going

00:20:40.290 --> 00:20:42.540
to cancel, we could
[? say that. ?]

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:46.890
So I put a proverb, "Money
saved is money made,"

00:20:46.890 --> 00:20:48.720
or our subscribers
say it is money made.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:50.450
And I thought of
two more yesterday,

00:20:50.450 --> 00:20:52.510
so I'll tell you another one.

00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:56.610
So "Prevention is better
than cure," and "One in hand

00:20:56.610 --> 00:20:58.410
is better than two in the bush."

00:20:58.410 --> 00:21:00.870
So this just proves that
I passed my English test.

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:04.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:06.960
This gives you insights
into the future

00:21:06.960 --> 00:21:09.880
of cancellation of subscribers.

00:21:09.880 --> 00:21:14.820
It's two-class, so it's a
binary logistic regression,

00:21:14.820 --> 00:21:16.950
people who cancel
and who didn't.

00:21:16.950 --> 00:21:22.320
And we took one year of data
subscription, newsletter,

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:25.210
demographic, web browsing.

00:21:25.210 --> 00:21:27.210
All of that data, again,
is sitting in BigQuery.

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:36.210
So the architecture
is really simple.

00:21:36.210 --> 00:21:39.800
It's really two nodes,
BigQuery and Looker.

00:21:39.800 --> 00:21:42.760
But I put in a few
steps of what happens.

00:21:42.760 --> 00:21:48.120
So we're ETLing all our
datasets using Airflow, doing

00:21:48.120 --> 00:21:52.030
a little bit of preprocessing
of our Google Analytics data,

00:21:52.030 --> 00:21:56.130
like making some summary tables,
especially of how subscribers

00:21:56.130 --> 00:21:59.520
end up browsing.

00:21:59.520 --> 00:22:03.090
And then third step is the
real machine learning step.

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:07.020
You just do create
model, the model name,

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:09.360
and then you give
it a table with all

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:10.950
of the columns
with the features.

00:22:10.950 --> 00:22:14.250
And one column is
the label column.

00:22:14.250 --> 00:22:18.840
And the label in our case is,
did this person cancel or not?

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:20.760
And then when you
run this query,

00:22:20.760 --> 00:22:22.290
it takes about four
or five minutes.

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:27.260
You go and grab a cup of tea or
coffee, and when you're back,

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:31.420
the query number four
is you run select-star

00:22:31.420 --> 00:22:36.170
from Predict on a table which
has existing subscribers--

00:22:36.170 --> 00:22:38.040
existing current subscribers.

00:22:38.040 --> 00:22:42.580
And for those subscribers,
it will give you a score of--

00:22:42.580 --> 00:22:47.040
a probability score of how
likely they are to cancel.

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:49.470
And then, we built a bunch--

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:52.320
a dashboard and a
bunch of looks in it

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:55.200
which showed the
output of the results

00:22:55.200 --> 00:22:59.400
and also the output of our
machine learning matrix,

00:22:59.400 --> 00:23:02.445
such as ROC, or
precision and recall.

00:23:07.060 --> 00:23:11.750
So this is a snapshot
of a dashboard.

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:13.630
So the first look
is basically showing

00:23:13.630 --> 00:23:17.410
all the subscribers are
sorted by how likely they are

00:23:17.410 --> 00:23:20.350
to cancel their subscription.

00:23:20.350 --> 00:23:25.570
And somebody in the
subscription retention team

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:28.450
can take a look
at it and at least

00:23:28.450 --> 00:23:31.330
have an idea of what
this is predicting,

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:34.150
like do some forecasting.

00:23:34.150 --> 00:23:36.890
This number on the right
side is the AUC curve.

00:23:36.890 --> 00:23:40.280
It's called Area
Under the Curve score.

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:46.390
And it's essentially the
area under this other graph.

00:23:46.390 --> 00:23:50.560
And it's a data sciencey
thing, but what it means

00:23:50.560 --> 00:23:53.860
is that if this AUC
score was 50%, then

00:23:53.860 --> 00:23:56.350
our machine learning model
hadn't learned anything,

00:23:56.350 --> 00:23:59.260
meaning that this
line over here that's

00:23:59.260 --> 00:24:02.320
a lot of false positive
versus true positive

00:24:02.320 --> 00:24:05.300
would just be a horizontal line.

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:08.830
But this shows A, that our data
has predictive power and two,

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:13.780
that BQML is learning something.

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:16.550
And on the right, we're
plotting the same graph,

00:24:16.550 --> 00:24:19.940
which is the true positive rate
and the false positive rate.

00:24:19.940 --> 00:24:21.880
So another problem
you have to figure out

00:24:21.880 --> 00:24:27.790
is what probability
threshold above which

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:29.860
you say that this person
is a churner or not.

00:24:29.860 --> 00:24:33.980
Do you say like 50%
above or 30% and above?

00:24:33.980 --> 00:24:37.450
So this graph over here on
the bottom on the right side

00:24:37.450 --> 00:24:42.850
shows a plot of true positive
and false positive rate.

00:24:42.850 --> 00:24:44.650
And we want to have
a threshold which

00:24:44.650 --> 00:24:48.580
gives a decent true
positive and also

00:24:48.580 --> 00:24:51.200
have low false positive rate.

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:56.120
So we chose about 0.3,
which gives us about 18%

00:24:56.120 --> 00:24:59.680
false positive and 80%
true positive rate, which

00:24:59.680 --> 00:25:00.670
I feel is fine.

00:25:00.670 --> 00:25:04.150
Because even if
you send out emails

00:25:04.150 --> 00:25:07.570
to people who you think
are going to churn

00:25:07.570 --> 00:25:10.360
and if they don't end up
churning, it doesn't hurt.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:11.830
You just sent out extra emails.

00:25:15.100 --> 00:25:17.980
So you can also get the
weights of your learned model

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:20.500
to get an idea of
what it's learning.

00:25:20.500 --> 00:25:24.120
So this is a plot of the
weights of [? this thing. ?]

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:26.860
So the features
that are on the left

00:25:26.860 --> 00:25:29.380
are positively
correlated to churn,

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:31.870
while the features on
the right are negatively

00:25:31.870 --> 00:25:33.550
correlated to churn.

00:25:33.550 --> 00:25:36.910
And it gives us a sense that,
let's look at this feature

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:41.470
there might be some clue
in our historical data

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:44.590
that we might be able to
make some business decisions

00:25:44.590 --> 00:25:48.460
or change the way how
we're doing business

00:25:48.460 --> 00:25:51.040
and reduce our churn.

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:52.670
And it has two
types of features.

00:25:52.670 --> 00:25:54.850
One is just numerical features.

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:57.820
And the others are
categorical features.

00:25:57.820 --> 00:26:01.030
So you have to do a UNNEST
in your BigQuery SQL

00:26:01.030 --> 00:26:04.600
to be able to get
those other features.

00:26:04.600 --> 00:26:07.240
And you can look up
at the BQML tutorials

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:11.200
with examples of other use
cases to do predictive modeling.

00:26:14.450 --> 00:26:20.740
So AutoML text was released
in this conference.

00:26:20.740 --> 00:26:24.070
So while this was
in alpha, I've been

00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:27.190
working with the product
manager and built

00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:30.340
a model for ourselves.

00:26:30.340 --> 00:26:33.040
So we have two main
use cases that--

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:37.960
the discussion about DFP,
being able to match ads.

00:26:37.960 --> 00:26:40.970
We also want to enhance
some of our categories.

00:26:40.970 --> 00:26:43.360
For example, we take the
sensitive subject category

00:26:43.360 --> 00:26:46.540
and break it down into
more granular ones.

00:26:46.540 --> 00:26:49.630
Or like if there is
a new sport that's

00:26:49.630 --> 00:26:52.780
not covered by the
default categories,

00:26:52.780 --> 00:26:54.490
we want to train our own.

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:56.210
So that's one use case.

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.910
The other use cases
that I worked over here

00:26:58.910 --> 00:27:02.410
is to be able to detect
evergreen content.

00:27:02.410 --> 00:27:07.810
Evergreen content is content
that has a longer lifespan.

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.810
For example, a review of
a restaurant or a museum,

00:27:10.810 --> 00:27:13.780
we'll call it evergreen content.

00:27:13.780 --> 00:27:19.810
But a story about some accident
that happened at a mall,

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:23.580
it only has a few days
or a few weeks' life.

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:26.940
So we want to be able
to differentiate content

00:27:26.940 --> 00:27:28.750
that's evergreen or not.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:33.610
So initially, I tried this open
source dataset from StumbleUpon

00:27:33.610 --> 00:27:35.350
from Gaggle.

00:27:35.350 --> 00:27:38.500
Very initially, I tried
to write my own TensorFlow

00:27:38.500 --> 00:27:40.090
code using LSTM.

00:27:40.090 --> 00:27:45.350
I just quickly prototyped
it, felt that it--

00:27:45.350 --> 00:27:46.840
the data had predictive power.

00:27:46.840 --> 00:27:49.720
But I had some hunch
that Google was

00:27:49.720 --> 00:27:52.080
working on something like this.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:27:59.500
So then, we created a
dataset of our own dataset

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:01.600
to label evergreen content.

00:28:01.600 --> 00:28:03.730
So it was sort of
like internally

00:28:03.730 --> 00:28:09.000
crowdsourced through to our CMS,
where editors basically tagged

00:28:09.000 --> 00:28:13.370
some of this content
as evergreen.

00:28:13.370 --> 00:28:15.590
And to use AutoML
is really easy.

00:28:15.590 --> 00:28:19.220
You create a CSV file,
give it first column

00:28:19.220 --> 00:28:23.020
as the content and
the second column

00:28:23.020 --> 00:28:26.090
as the label Evergreen
or Not Evergreen,

00:28:26.090 --> 00:28:30.380
and then upload it to
AutoML and have it run--

00:28:30.380 --> 00:28:32.930
learn from this data.

00:28:32.930 --> 00:28:34.100
So you see this--

00:28:34.100 --> 00:28:39.350
we had about 3,000 articles
for evergreen and another 3,000

00:28:39.350 --> 00:28:41.120
for non-evergreen.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:43.120
And you see it has
done a pretty good job

00:28:43.120 --> 00:28:44.130
in learning this thing.

00:28:44.130 --> 00:28:49.070
I was surprised that
it could do so well.

00:28:49.070 --> 00:28:51.170
And you see the
precision recall score.

00:28:51.170 --> 00:28:54.260
And it also shows you
the confusion metrics

00:28:54.260 --> 00:28:57.780
of what it predicted.

00:28:57.780 --> 00:29:01.520
91% of the time
non-evergreen content,

00:29:01.520 --> 00:29:02.900
it predicted as non-evergreen.

00:29:02.900 --> 00:29:05.480
And only 9% of the
time it made a mistake

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:08.110
for non-evergreen content.

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:11.740
And for evergreen,
it did a perfect job.

00:29:11.740 --> 00:29:16.160
And this is only on
their test set database.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:18.920
So I took this, and
I played around.

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:23.080
In the AutoML console, you
can put in any random text

00:29:23.080 --> 00:29:26.560
and see how your
prediction model does.

00:29:26.560 --> 00:29:29.550
So I tried it on
our Hearst articles.

00:29:29.550 --> 00:29:32.140
I tried it on CNN articles.

00:29:32.140 --> 00:29:34.370
And it seemed to work very well.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:36.670
And then I even
went to Wikipedia.

00:29:36.670 --> 00:29:39.250
So I picked up an
article about New York

00:29:39.250 --> 00:29:42.290
so-- that you
think is evergreen.

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:47.710
And 70%, it predicted
this is evergreen.

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:51.490
And then again from
Wikipedia, I picked an article

00:29:51.490 --> 00:29:56.710
about Mexico elections, and it
predicted it as non-evergreen.

00:29:56.710 --> 00:29:59.990
So it's basically the combined
knowledge of [? editorials ?]

00:29:59.990 --> 00:30:02.320
and what they think is
evergreen and non-evergreen

00:30:02.320 --> 00:30:08.050
this AutoML has learned and
is able to apply on text

00:30:08.050 --> 00:30:09.400
that it hasn't seen before.

00:30:16.370 --> 00:30:21.600
So ideally, we want to
use BigQuery for most

00:30:21.600 --> 00:30:24.090
of our analytic process.

00:30:24.090 --> 00:30:28.670
Anything that can be
formulated as SQL,

00:30:28.670 --> 00:30:33.220
I tend to want to use BigQuery,
because it's distributed.

00:30:33.220 --> 00:30:37.500
And it's more cost effective
to do things in BigQuery.

00:30:37.500 --> 00:30:41.160
But there are some
use cases that it's--

00:30:41.160 --> 00:30:44.070
like very few use cases that
you have to do things outside

00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:45.810
of BigQuery.

00:30:45.810 --> 00:30:49.280
So one of the use cases that
we have duplicate content,

00:30:49.280 --> 00:30:53.430
content which either the
body looks very similar

00:30:53.430 --> 00:30:55.890
or the headline is tweaked,
and essentially it's

00:30:55.890 --> 00:30:57.690
the same article.

00:30:57.690 --> 00:31:00.990
And we don't want to
recommend articles

00:31:00.990 --> 00:31:06.960
that are similar to each other
in a list of recommendations.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:08.880
And what we wanted
to do initially

00:31:08.880 --> 00:31:13.180
was a big read like create
a word to each article.

00:31:13.180 --> 00:31:17.910
And then do a cross
join with itself

00:31:17.910 --> 00:31:22.800
and figure out the distance
between those articles.

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:25.620
And doing this in
BigQuery was not possible

00:31:25.620 --> 00:31:28.930
because that query
wouldn't return.

00:31:28.930 --> 00:31:30.820
So what we did is
in this use case,

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:33.310
we used Spark,
basically spawned up

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:38.310
a cluster of 10 machines
for a couple of hours.

00:31:38.310 --> 00:31:44.490
And we wrote up by Spark
job to compute these vectors

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:47.790
and basically compute the cosine
distance between articles.

00:31:47.790 --> 00:31:50.070
And articles that had--

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:52.260
were very close
to each other, we

00:31:52.260 --> 00:31:55.800
eliminated them as duplicates.

00:31:55.800 --> 00:31:58.260
This helps our
recommendation system

00:31:58.260 --> 00:32:03.240
to remove any duplicate
content and a use case of Spark

00:32:03.240 --> 00:32:06.315
and DataProc in our data.

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:13.020
So I'm getting close--

00:32:13.020 --> 00:32:14.390
this is actually my last line.

00:32:14.390 --> 00:32:20.330
So what's the future
of Hearst Newspapers?

00:32:20.330 --> 00:32:23.540
So we want to build
more predictive models

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:25.940
using BigQuery and ML.

00:32:25.940 --> 00:32:29.790
Because it makes a lot of sense
to since have all the data,

00:32:29.790 --> 00:32:32.900
and it's very easy
to use BigQuery ML.

00:32:32.900 --> 00:32:36.080
Things like propensity
modeling, like the reverse

00:32:36.080 --> 00:32:38.450
of churn modeling,
is to figure out,

00:32:38.450 --> 00:32:42.200
who are the subscribers that
are likely to subscribe?

00:32:42.200 --> 00:32:44.660
Or who are the visitors
on our web pages

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:48.140
who are likely to subscribe?

00:32:48.140 --> 00:32:52.550
And this could help our
marketing systems focus

00:32:52.550 --> 00:32:54.310
on those.

00:32:54.310 --> 00:32:58.220
And actually in
yesterday's BQML demo,

00:32:58.220 --> 00:33:01.820
the demo was about figuring
out those people that

00:33:01.820 --> 00:33:06.560
are likely future customers.

00:33:06.560 --> 00:33:10.400
We want to productionalize the
taxonomy I was talking about.

00:33:10.400 --> 00:33:13.600
We already have
requirements to--

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:16.880
in AutoML, we want to
create our own datasets

00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:22.820
and train it to enhance
the NLP taxonomy.

00:33:22.820 --> 00:33:25.910
There's been lots of research
using deep neural networks

00:33:25.910 --> 00:33:28.680
for recommendation systems.

00:33:28.680 --> 00:33:31.220
There's actually
one article which

00:33:31.220 --> 00:33:33.680
lists all the different
research papers

00:33:33.680 --> 00:33:36.980
using deep neural networks
for recommendation system.

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:42.070
And we've been prototyping
a few different approaches.

00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:43.460
There's hybrid approaches.

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:47.390
There's all sorts of different
flavors of doing recommendation

00:33:47.390 --> 00:33:50.520
systems using deep learning.

00:33:50.520 --> 00:33:55.100
So that research is continuing,
and hopefully, our version two

00:33:55.100 --> 00:33:59.990
will be a more-- even more
advanced recommendation system.

00:33:59.990 --> 00:34:04.860
There's still more juice to
be taken out from Google NLP,

00:34:04.860 --> 00:34:08.330
that we haven't fully
utilized the NLP,

00:34:08.330 --> 00:34:11.570
especially some of
the use cases are

00:34:11.570 --> 00:34:16.550
being able to build personalized
newsletters or topic

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:18.980
pages using Google NLP.

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:22.610
So we are thinking
on these other use

00:34:22.610 --> 00:34:24.980
cases and how to
productionalize this.

00:34:24.980 --> 00:34:29.120
And also, we have a
large corpus of images--

00:34:29.120 --> 00:34:33.750
haven't yet reached that point
to build a product around it.

00:34:33.750 --> 00:34:38.910
So that's one of the things
that I have for the future.

00:34:38.910 --> 00:34:42.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

